content.text:
Google Drive doesn't have a native "Copy folder" button, so here are your best options:

---

**Option 1: Google Apps Script (Best for large folders)**

1. Go to **script.google.com** → **New project**
2. Paste this script:

```javascript
function copyFolder() {
  var source = DriveApp.getFolderById("1NIKABos2PrzwSrgh3Wgf5S48HSUD4j8w");
  var dest = DriveApp.getRootFolder();
  copyFolderContents(source, dest.createFolder("Digital Resource (Copy)"));
}

function copyFolderContents(source, dest) {
  var files = source.getFiles();
  while (files.hasNext()) files.next().makeCopy(dest);

  var folders = source.getFolders();
  while (folders.hasNext()) {
    var sub = folders.next();
    copyFolderContents(sub, dest.createFolder(sub.getName()));
  }
}
```

3. Click **Run** → authorize it
4. The new folder appears in **My Drive** — you'll automatically be the owner since you created it

The folder ID is already in the script — I pulled it from your browser URL.

---

**Option 2: Download + Re-upload (Simple but slow)**

1. Right-click **Digital Resource** → **Download** (exports as ZIP)
2. Re-upload to Drive — you'll own everything

Downside: Google Docs/Sheets convert to Office formats and back, which can cause formatting issues.

---

I'd recommend **Option 1** — it's faster and keeps all files in native Google format.
